{{Politics of Balisca}}

A '''confederal emergency''' ({{lang-bl|Emergencia confederal}}) is a [[W:State of emergency|situation]] that is determined to require [[W:Collective security|collective cooperation and organization at the confederal level]], allowing for the centralization of the [[Confederal Armed Forces|armed forces]] under [[Government and politics of Balisca|confederal authority]]. There have been x confederal emergencies since the 1808 [[Proclamation of Alcazaba]], most recently in [[South Atlantic War|1976]] and [[Gulf of Guinea Crisis|1977]] (both were terminated in 1983).

Balisca declared an "indefinite" [[W:Climate emergency declaration|climate emergency]] in 1985. The debate intensified during the administration of [[Cristiano Sandoval]], which argued that worsening [[W:climate change|climate change]] constituted a confederal emergency and justified the composition of a confederal defense force capable of responding to the [[W:Effects of climate change|effects of climate change]]. Others have rejected this, arguing that the increasing sophistication of confederal forces contributes to the international [[W:Arms industry|arms industry]] and the [[W:Militarization|militarization of Balisca]], the latter of which could be weaponized against the [[Cantons of Balisca|cantons]] and [[Communes of Balisca|communes]], and potentially violate constitutional protections.

